                   REVISED — no custom crate mesh/animation after all. Turns out vanilla BuildBox
                   crates have no per-module mesh or animation whatsoever: BuildBoxActor.Awake()
                   (sits on the SAME GameObject as BuildBox — AbstractObjectActor<T>.Object is just
                   GetComponent<T>()) discards whatever's on the prefab and rebuilds the crate at
                   runtime from ONE shared BuildBoxVisualSettings ScriptableObject, keyed purely by
                   BuildSize and tinted by category — every module in the game, vanilla included,
                   shares it. So instead of modeling/exporting our own crate (animation-loss risk on
                   export, plus not wanting ripped game assets), AssetLoader.EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired
                   grafts a real BuildBoxActor onto our prefab and wires its two private fields
                   (buildBoxVisualSettings, diodes) via reflection off a live vanilla donor — a
                   CellModule.BuildBoxRef found on the player's own ship, resolved through
                   ResourcePaths + Resources.Load (same path CustomObjectPool itself uses for vanilla
                   GUIDs). Deferred out of LoadBundle/GraftBuildBoxComponents (plugin Awake, too early
                   — ResourcePaths/ship modules don't exist yet, same trap as the PhotonNetwork-at-Awake
                   bug) to a lazy, idempotent call at both spawn sites (ForgeSpawnCommand,
                   BossDefeatHook.AwardForgeBuildBox) — one-time lookup, not per-spawn, so it shouldn't
                   repeat the old TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid lag spike. GraftBuildBoxComponents also now
                   grafts a generated BoxCollider (0.5m cube) since nothing authored provides one
                   anymore — Grabbable.Collider is just a lazy GetComponent<Collider>().
