                    ✔ FIXED (26-08-15) — !forgespawn crashed every time (NullReferenceException,
                       confirmed via in-game LogOutput.log): ObjectFactory.InstantiateSpaceObjectByGUID
                       unconditionally looks the guid up in the VANILLA CloneStarObjectContainer and
                       dereferences the result with no null check. Our BuildBox guid only exists in
                       RuntimeAssetsRegister (AssetLoader deliberately bypasses the vanilla
                       RuntimeAssetConverter for module-shaped prefabs the SDK can't convert — see
                       AssetLoader.LoadBundle's doc comment), so that lookup always came back null.
                       ForgeSpawnCommand now calls PhotonNetwork.Instantiate("#"+guid) directly
                       (same thing CustomObjectPool does under the hood, minus the broken vanilla
                       lookup) and replicates the two harmless steps by hand (assetGuid,
                       ObjectRegistry.RegisterObject).
                       ⚠ At the time: same root cause almost certainly breaks BuildBox.BuildModule
                       (vanilla, CG.Ship.Object/BuildBox.cs) too — the actual "carry box to socket,
                       ship builds the module" gameplay flow, since it calls the exact same
                       ObjectFactory.InstantiateSpaceObjectByGUID with moduleRef.AssetGuid. Superseded
                       by the proper fix below (RegisterRuntimeAsset) — see that entry.

                    ✔ FIXED (26-08-15) — second bug found via live LogOutput.log after the first fix:
                       box spawned with no crash but was completely invisible/non-interactable
                       ("nothing there", confirmed by walking to the spot). Real cause:
                       Client.Ship.Actor.BuildBoxActor.Awake() → GetMeshSetup() NREs on
                       ((CellModule)moduleRef.Asset).BuildingConstraints because moduleRef.Asset
                       returns null. Why: CloneStarObjectRef.IsRuntime is [NonSerialized] — Unity's
                       prefab→instance clone (what PhotonNetwork.Instantiate does under the hood)
                       does NOT carry NonSerialized fields across, so AssetLoader.LinkForgeBuildBox's
                       one-time `moduleRef.IsRuntime = true` on the PREFAB asset is invisible on every
                       spawned INSTANCE (silently resets to false), so Asset falls through to a
                       vanilla ResourcePaths/Resources.Load lookup that can never find our guid.
                       Fixed with a new Harmony patch, Patches/BuildBoxRuntimeRefPatch.cs — a Prefix
                       on BuildBoxActor.Awake that re-stamps moduleRef.IsRuntime = true right before
                       Awake reads it, gated to only guids RuntimeAssetsRegister actually knows about
                       (vanilla BuildBox instances untouched). Since BuildBox.BuildModule/GetBuildSize
                       (the real "install into ship socket" flow) read this exact same moduleRef.Asset,
                       this ALSO covers the ⚠ concern above about the install flow — same broken field,
                       same fix, one patch. Still worth an explicit VERIFY on the install flow itself
                       though (not yet tested standalone).
                       Minor/non-blocking leftover: the log also showed
                       "Cannot instantiate objects with a parent which is persistent" once, from
                       EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired's Object.Instantiate(donorDiodes, boxPrefab.transform)
                       — Unity refuses to parent a runtime instance under an AssetBundle-loaded
                       (persistent) prefab transform and falls back to no parent instead. The diodes
                       clone still ends up correctly referenced (BuildBoxActor.Awake repositions it via
                       transform.position regardless of parent), so this is a one-time scene-root leak,
                       not a functional bug — fine to leave, worth tidying later if it gets noisy.

                    ✔ FIXED (26-08-15) — third bug: box spawned WITH visuals this time (confirms the
                       IsRuntime fix above worked) but fell straight through the ship floor — "no
                       colliders" as reported, even though GraftBuildBoxComponents does add one. Real
                       cause, found in the same log: a KeyNotFoundException fires every single
                       RepeatEventScheduler.FixedUpdate tick, from CarryableObject.UpdateAtmosphereData
                       → SetPlatform → UpdateSector → GameSessionSector.AddOrbitObject → ... →
                       CsObjectReference.IsInstanceOf → ResourceAssetContainer<CloneStarObjectContainer,
                       ...>.GetItem(guid) — a bare Dictionary[] lookup with no TryGetValue, no
                       fallback to RuntimeAssetsRegister. That's the SAME "our guid only lives in
                       RuntimeAssetsRegister, vanilla code assumes CloneStarObjectContainer" gap as the
                       ObjectFactory crash fixed above, just a different vanilla consumer of it — and
                       since it's uncaught and fires every physics tick from inside the exact call
                       chain that connects a CarryableObject to the ship's platform/simulation, it was
                       plausibly aborting that connection before it completed each frame — consistent
                       with "has a real collider but falls through the floor anyway."
                       Rather than patch each individual vanilla call site that does this (whack-a-mole
                       — who knows how many more there are), used the container's own first-class API
                       for exactly this situation: ResourceAssetContainer<U,T,V>.RegisterRuntimeAsset
                       (adds to both RuntimeDescriptions and the internal assetDefLUT dictionary, no
                       reflection needed — it's public). New AssetLoader.EnsureRuntimeAssetsRegisteredInVanillaContainers,
                       called as the first step of EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired (same lazy/deferred/once
                       timing — ResourceAssetContainerRegister isn't populated at plugin Awake either):
                       registers both the module and BuildBox prefab guids into CloneStarObjectContainer
                       with IsRuntime = true on the def's Ref (safe here — unlike the Awake case, this
                       CloneStarObjectDef is a plain C# object that never goes through Unity's
                       prefab-clone pipeline, so IsNonSerialized doesn't reset it). This should fix the
                       falling-through-floor AND make BuildBox.BuildModule's own
                       ObjectFactory.InstantiateSpaceObjectByGUID call succeed without needing
                       ForgeSpawnCommand's bypass pattern repeated elsewhere.

                       RETESTED — CloneStarObjectContainer registration confirmed working (log is
