                       ARCHITECTURE CHANGE (user's suggestion, and the right call) — stop trying to
                       reconstruct a working BuildBox component-by-component. Instead: spawn a REAL
                       vanilla BuildBox (found once via a live CellModule.BuildBoxRef, cached — new
                       AssetLoader.TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid) and relabel its moduleRef to point at
                       the Forge module, instead of instantiating our own custom-grafted prefab. A
                       real vanilla BuildBox already has 100% correct physics/simulation/rendering —
                       cloning one sidesteps the whole mystery instead of solving it.
                       This IS the original donor-borrowing approach from before the dedicated
                       BuildBox existed (see git history) — reintroduced because the dedicated
                       prefab turned out to never actually work, but this time the donor guid is
                       found ONCE and cached, not re-scanned per spawn (a per-spawn scan of the whole
                       module registry was the cause of the ORIGINAL !forgespawn lag spike that
                       motivated moving away from donors — that risk doesn't apply to a cached
                       one-time lookup).
                       FIRST ATTEMPT (superseded, see below): ForgeSpawnCommand spawned the donor's
                       OWN guid directly via SpawnUtils.SpawnCarryable, then overrode box.moduleRef
                       on the resulting live instance afterward. Fixed the falling — confirmed no
                       longer falls through the floor — BUT the box then had no hover label, couldn't
                       be placed or dropped, and was held wrong. Cause: BuildBoxActor.Awake() (which
                       sizes/colors the crate from moduleRef.Asset) — and evidently other systems too
                       (hover label, placement validation, carry-hold positioning) — already ran
                       against the DONOR's ORIGINAL moduleRef by the time our post-spawn override
                       happened, leaving the box in an inconsistent half-donor-half-Forge state. Not
                       a cosmetic-only gap after all — genuinely broke core interaction.

                       FIX — preset moduleRef on the TEMPLATE, before any real instance's Awake ever
                       runs, instead of relabeling a live instance after spawn. Required restructuring
                       how the BuildBox's stable GUID gets registered, since RuntimeAssetsRegister /
                       CloneStarObjectContainer only support register-once (no update API):
                        - LoadBundle no longer grafts/registers the BuildBox bundle prefab at all —
                          only captures its stamped VoidCrewAsset guid (AssetLoader._buildBoxGuid).
                          That prefab's SHAPE is now unused entirely.
                        - New AssetLoader.EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady() (replaces
                          EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired): finds the donor (TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid,
                          still cached/one-time), clones it WHILE THE DONOR IS MOMENTARILY INACTIVE
                          (donorPrefab.SetActive(false) → Instantiate → SetActive(true) — mirrors
                          exactly what CustomObjectPool.Instantiate itself does around every real
                          spawn, so the clone starts inactive too and Awake never fires on the
                          template itself — it's a pure inert data-template, same as our original
                          bundle-loaded prefab was), sets the clone's moduleRef.AssetGuid to the Forge
                          module's guid (survives future clone-of-the-template just fine — it's a
                          normal serializable field, not [NonSerialized]), and registers THIS CLONE
                          (fresh — first-ever registration for our BuildBox guid, no TryAdd conflict)
                          into both RuntimeAssetsRegister and CloneStarObjectContainer.
                        - LinkForgeBuildBox split into LinkForgeBuildBoxRef (module → box direction
                          only, unchanged timing at LoadBundle) — the box → module direction moved
                          into EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady above, since it now acts on the template,
                          which doesn't exist until the donor is found.
                        - ForgeSpawnCommand simplified back to the ordinary shape: call
                          EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady(), look up OUR guid (not the donor's), spawn via
                          SpawnUtils.SpawnCarryable(ourGuid, ...). No post-spawn moduleRef override —
                          it's already correct from Awake() onward on every instance.
                        - BossDefeatHook switched from EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired to
                          EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady too — and since it already spawns via OUR
                          registered guid (through the care-package payload), it gets the same fix
                          automatically, no separate relabeling logic needed there at all.
                        - Deconstruct (CellModule.BuildBoxRef, read by vanilla
                          Deconstruct.CreateBuildBox) also already points at OUR guid via
                          LinkForgeBuildBoxRef — once EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady has run (guaranteed
                          by the time deconstruction is even possible, since installing the module in
                          the first place requires a box to have already been spawned), that guid
                          resolves to the correct template too. Likely fixed as a side effect of this
                          same change — NOT CONFIRMED, no dedicated test done.
                       Patches/BuildBoxRuntimeRefPatch.cs (the Awake-time IsRuntime re-stamp) is still
                       required and unchanged — moduleRef.IsRuntime is [NonSerialized] regardless of
                       which prefab/template it's set on, so every spawned INSTANCE still needs it
                       re-stamped right before Awake reads it.
                       This also removes ALL of EnsureBuildBoxVisualsWired's reflection into the
                       shared vanilla BuildBoxVisualSettings asset (the prime suspect for the
                       "all BuildBoxes turned purple" regression) — replaced by a genuine unmodified
                       clone of a real donor, which references that shared asset completely normally
                       the same way any other vanilla BuildBox does. Never confirmed as the actual
                       cause, but the suspect code path no longer exists either way.

                       RETESTED — physics fix confirmed (no more falling), but new failure: after
                       re-adding the bare BuildBox marker prefab and retesting, !forgespawn threw
                       outright (Command Exception in SpawnUtils.SpawnCarryable) and the log flooded
                       with thousands of repeated NullReferenceExceptions from
                       CompositeWeaponBuildBox.GetBuildSize()/get_ContextInfo(). Root cause:
                       TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid took the FIRST live CellModule.BuildBoxRef it found
                       with no filtering — this time it happened to land on a composite/modular
                       weapon module, whose BuildBoxRef points at a CompositeWeaponBuildBox (a BuildBox
                       subclass that overrides GetBuildSize/ContextInfo to read WeaponDataRef instead
                       of moduleRef). Cloning one and setting moduleRef (this whole approach) leaves
                       WeaponDataRef null/empty, and every downstream system that reads those
                       overridden members — sector marker creation (breaks the spawn itself), hover
                       context info, per-frame socket-highlight outlines — NREs. This is almost
                       certainly what was ACTUALLY behind the earlier "no hover label / can't
                       place-or-drop / held wrong" report too (FindObjectsOfType iteration order isn't
                       guaranteed stable, so which CellModule got picked as donor could vary run to
                       run) — not a leftover Awake-timing issue as originally guessed.
                       FIXED: TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid now loads each candidate's prefab and skips it
                       if its BuildBox component is (or is a) CompositeWeaponBuildBox, continuing the
                       scan for a plain-BuildBox donor instead.

                       REFINED (user's suggestion) — instead of just excluding weapon donors and
                       accepting whichever plain BuildBox came first in scene-iteration order,
                       TryFindDonorBuildBoxGuid now actively PREFERS a donor whose own module is
                       tagged Utility (Module_Category_Utility — the same CsTag ForgeAttachHelper
                       already stamps onto the Forge module itself, see that method), so the borrowed
                       crate's own context-info label reads as "Utility Build Box" — the right kind
                       for what the Forge thematically is — rather than an arbitrary category. Falls
                       back to any plain non-weapon BuildBox if no Utility-tagged module happens to be
                       installed on this particular ship.
                    CONFIRMED (user): !forgespawn now spawns correctly — box stays put and has a
                    hover label. Only remaining issue reported: hover showed "missing description."
                       ROOT CAUSE: EnsureBuildBoxTemplateReady registers the crate's own guid via a
                       freshly-constructed CloneStarObjectDef, which leaves its private `contextInfo`
                       field null. CloneStarObjectDef.ContextInfo (get) falls back to
                       DataTable<DefaultAssetTable>.Instance.MissingContextInfo whenever that field is
                       null — that fallback IS the "missing description" text, not a bug in the hover
                       system itself.
