                 NEW BUG (user): deconstructing an unrelated module (a "scoop module") instead
                 deconstructed the Forge module.
                    INVESTIGATED: no code path in this mod redirects Deconstruct/BuildProcessController
                    to the wrong CellModule — ForgeStateStore is keyed by CellModule reference
                    (ConditionalWeakTable, correct identity), LinkForgeBuildBoxRef only ever touches
                    the Forge's own module prefab (confirmed by the log: "[AssetLoader] Linked Forge
                    module -> BuildBox ref ..." fires exactly once, scoped to UpgradeForgeModuleCell),
                    and ForgeAttachHelper.TryAttach's IsForgeModule check is tag/name gated so it
                    can't misfire onto a vanilla module. The first log the user provided didn't cover
                    the incident at all (ends at leaving the room, no deconstruct activity — and none
                    of our deconstruct-adjacent patches log anything anyway, so it wouldn't have shown
                    up even if it had happened during that session).
                    LEADING THEORY: the Forge's own deconstruct handle (ForgeDeconstructInteractable)
                    uses a raycast-driven click region (BuildAnchorClickRegion), same mechanism vanilla
                    modules' own ExtruderLever deconstruct levers use (both ultimately resolve through
                    RaycastHandler.Current). If the Handle anchor lacks its own authored Collider in
                    the Unity source, BuildAnchorClickRegion falls back to a generated BoxCollider sized
                    by dividing the requested world-space size (0.2,0.2,0.2) by the anchor's lossyScale
                    — if that scale is small (FBX import quirk), the resulting collider could balloon
                    far past the Handle's visual mesh and physically steal raycasts aimed at a
                    neighboring module's own deconstruct lever if the Forge is placed nearby.
                    INSTRUMENTED: BuildAnchorClickRegion LogDebug's, for every Forge click region
                    built, which branch fired (authored vs generated) and the resulting
                    Collider.bounds (center + size) in world space.
                    ROOT CAUSE FOUND (user, same session): the prefab's deconstruct anchor was never
                    actually named "Handle" — user split it into two objects, "Handle" (a purely
                    cosmetic part of the modeled mesh, buried in the FBX hierarchy so it never shows
                    up as its own YAML block when grepping the .prefab) and "DeconstructTrigger" (a
                    dedicated, hand-authored-and-sized SphereCollider added specifically as the click
                    target). UpgradeForgeBehavior.DeconstructHandleName == "Handle" still matched
                    only the cosmetic part, which has no Collider of its own and no "ClickTarget"
                    child either — so BuildAnchorClickRegion always fell into its generated-box
                    fallback for it. This is exactly the oversized-collider mechanism the leading
                    theory predicted, just discovered via the naming mismatch rather than the log.
                    FIXED: split the concepts in code, matching the split the user already made in
                    Unity. UpgradeForgeBehavior now looks up both "Handle" and "DeconstructTrigger"
                    separately; CreateDeconstructInteractable builds the click region from
                    DeconstructTrigger (which has its own authored Collider, so
                    BuildAnchorClickRegion's authored branch fires reliably — the generated fallback
                    is no longer reachable here at all) and passes Handle through as
                    ForgeDeconstructInteractable.VisualHandle, which the lever-pull animation now
                    rotates instead of rotating the click region's own transform. Handle is optional
                    (deconstruct still functions with no lever animation if absent); DeconstructTrigger
                    is required (no trigger anchor → deconstruct unavailable, logged).
                    STILL REPRODUCES (user, same session): with DeconstructTrigger wired as the click
                    region (no generated fallback in play at all), holding deconstruct on ANY OTHER
                    module still deconstructs the Forge too — and no interaction prompt shows for the
                    other module while this happens, until the Forge is gone, then things return to
                    normal. This rules out the oversized-collider theory outright (there's no
                    generated collider left in the picture) — the bug is not spatial/raycast-target
                    related at all.
                    ROOT CAUSE (found by reading the actual vanilla interaction source,
                    HoldClickerInteractable.cs / EnvironmentInteract.cs): HoldClickerInteractable.
                    StartClick() subscribes OnHoldCompleted onto a SINGLE GLOBAL InputAction
                    (ServiceBase<InputService>.Instance.InputActionReferences.HoldAction) — the same
                    action object shared by EVERY Hold-driven interactable in the game, vanilla
                    included — and only unsubscribes in EndClick() or once OnHoldCompleted itself
                    fires (self-removing). EnvironmentInteract.TryEndInteract() has a latent bug on
                    top of this: `if (!(currentInteractable == null)) { ... EndClick(); currentInteractable
                    = null; }` — if currentInteractable is a Unity "fake-null" (destroyed) reference,
                    `== null` is true via Unity's overload, so the whole block — INCLUDING the
                    EndClick() call — is skipped. If the Forge's DeconstructTrigger GameObject is ever
                    destroyed while it's still the tracked currentInteractable (plausible during this
                    kind of iterative in-session testing), its Hold subscription leaks permanently:
                    every SUBSEQUENT Hold completion anywhere in the game — including holding another
                    module's own deconstruct lever — invokes it too, deconstructing the Forge
                    regardless of what the player was actually targeting. ForgeCommitInteractable has
                    the identical structural risk (same base class, same subscribe pattern) even
                    though it hasn't been reported misfiring yet.
                    FIXED, two-part defense (neither requires pinning the exact leak trigger, both are
                    safe regardless of cause):
                      1. ForgeDeconstructInteractable/ForgeCommitInteractable now override OnDestroy()
                         to force-call EndClick() (which unconditionally unsubscribes from the shared
                         Hold action regardless of isClickable state) — plugs the leak at its most
                         likely source (component destroyed mid-hold).
                      2. OnDeconstruct()/OnCommit() now gate on this component's OWN locally-tracked
                         _holding bool (already existed for Deconstruct's rotation animation, added
                         fresh for Commit) before acting — so even a stray leaked callback that slips
                         past fix #1 becomes a no-op instead of touching the Forge, since _holding is
                         only ever true between THIS component's own StartClick/EndClick pair.
                    STILL REPRODUCES (user, same session, log captured but doesn't cover the incident —
                    log ends at room-leave/hot-reload teardown, same gap as every previous attempt: our
                    own deconstruct code logged NOTHING when it actually fired, so the log is silent on
                    the one thing that matters). User's refined description: merely LOOKING AT the
                    Forge's DeconstructTrigger, then looking away to a DIFFERENT module and trying to
                    deconstruct THAT one, still deconstructs the Forge — "like the prompt is still
                    there." This is a stronger, more specific repro than "any hold anywhere" and doesn't
                    obviously require an actual completed Hold on the Forge first — raises a live
                    alternative theory alongside the leaked-global-Hold-subscription one: EnvironmentInteract.
                    currentInteractable itself could be getting stuck pointed at the Forge's interactable
                    (RaycastHandler.Current not updating away from it), in which case _holding on the
                    Forge's own component would be legitimately (not spuriously) true when a later hold
                    completes elsewhere — meaning the fix #2 guard wouldn't catch it either, since from
                    the guard's perspective it looks like a real, correctly-scoped hold.
                    INSTRUMENTED (not yet confirmed either way): StartClick/EndClick on both
                    ForgeDeconstructInteractable and ForgeCommitInteractable now LogDebug their firing
                    (with GetInstanceID, to distinguish component instances across rebuilds) —
                    OnDeconstruct/OnCommit now LogInfo every invocation including _holding's value BEFORE
                    the guard check, so a leaked-but-guarded callback is now visible in the log instead
                    of invisible (previously: guard silently returns, nothing logged, no way to tell the
                    difference between "never fired" and "fired but correctly no-opped").
                    ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED (user, with log — instrumentation paid off): log showed
                    "Deconstruct StartClick ... GetInstanceID=-125338", then a BURST of 50+
                    "NullReferenceException ... void ClickerInteractable.Highlighted(bool) <-
                    AbstractInteractable.SetHighlighted <- RaycastHandler.SetHighlightedInteractable <-
                    RaycastHandler.RaycastInteractables <- PlayerRaycastUpdater.LateUpdate", then
                    "OnDeconstruct fired ... GetInstanceID=-125338, _holding=True" — SAME instance ID
                    as the StartClick. This DISPROVES the leaked-global-Hold-subscription theory: it was
                    a real, correctly-scoped hold the whole time, not a stray cross-fire.
                    ACTUAL BUG: ClickerInteractable.Highlighted (vanilla, what ForgeDeconstructInteractable
                    was inheriting unmodified — unlike ForgeCommitInteractable and ForgeInteractable,
                    which both already override it) iterates a private outlineObjects[] array that only
                    the Unity Inspector ever populates — null on every Forge interactable, since all are
                    AddComponent'd at runtime with no Inspector data behind them. Calling it NREs. This
                    fires from RaycastHandler.RaycastInteractables() every time the player's raycast
                    target changes to OR away from the trigger — and uncaught, that exception aborts the
                    REST of RaycastInteractables() for that frame, including whatever line reassigns
                    RaycastHandler.Current to the player's NEW target. So looking away from
                    DeconstructTrigger left RaycastHandler.Current permanently stuck pointing at it —
                    explaining BOTH halves of the report at once: no prompt on the other module (Current
                    never actually became the other module's interactable) AND the Forge deconstructing
                    instead (EnvironmentInteract.TryStartInteract reads the stuck Current regardless of
                    where the player is aiming, so StartClick/hold-completion legitimately run on the
                    Forge every time).
                    FIXED: ForgeDeconstructInteractable now overrides Highlighted the same way
                    ForgeCommitInteractable already does — a disabled "Highlight" child toggled on hover,
                    never touching ClickerInteractable.Highlighted (or its broken array) at all. The
                    earlier _holding-gate/OnDestroy defenses (added chasing the wrong theory) are kept —
                    harmless, and OnDestroy's force-unsubscribe is still cheap insurance against the
                    separate, real (if apparently not the actual trigger here) global-Hold-subscription
                    leak risk.
                    ✔ CONFIRMED (user): the core repro no longer reproduces — the
                    ClickerInteractable.Highlighted NRE / stuck-RaycastHandler.Current fix resolved it.
                    Deconstructing an unrelated module no longer touches the Forge. This closes out the
