   - small aux ship — RFC + design DONE @26-08-14, NOT SCHEDULED
      (docs/aux-fighter-rfc.html + docs/aux-fighter-design.html; vocabulary in
       handoff/CONTEXT.md under "Aux Fighter feature". Grilled 26-08-14; every
       open question below is now answered.)
      ✔ can only damage enemy fighters — guns damage vanilla Drone ONLY, hard rule.
         Survivability is NOT a damage rule: it's a small SignatureRadius, since
         TargetSelector scores candidates by (distance - SignatureRadius). Big ships
         ignore it while the main ship is in range; they engage it if it's close and
         the ship isn't. Per-enemy-type preference is a tuning knob. No AI patching.
      ✔ ejection on ship being destroyed — ALWAYS eject into EVA, same code path as
         the manual bail. Oxygen, distance, jetpack fuel and swarm EVA intercepts
         decide survival. Cockpit chair sets WorksWithJetpack=true so the pilot can
         fly wearing one.
      ✔ can be left behind — pilot dies, respawns at a clone tube, fighter lost.
         Same rule for undocking mid-jump. Hooks VoidJumpSystem.StateChanged.
      ✔ powered by relics? — NO. Alloys, at the bay panel. Relics are the Forge's
         only currency (16 relics L3→L10); a second relic sink would mean every
         sortie is a module upgrade forgone.
      ✔ must buy ship? — YES. Module installs EMPTY; fighter bought per bay with
         alloys. Hard cap of 2 fighters per run regardless of bays installed.
      ✔ docking — EXTERIOR hardpoint, NOT a hangar; nothing lands inside. The
         fighter clamps to the outside of the hull and its cockpit lines up with a
         hull pass-through (the "Docking Collar") so the cockpit sits adjacent to the
         module. The pilot does NOT walk in — they hold an interaction PROMPT and
         are seated, exactly like taking over a turret. No hull traversal, no EVA
         to board, so no character-pathing-across-the-hull risk at all. Modelled on CarryablesAirlock
         (CellModule whose CarryablesSockets are input+output, SocketState.Open),
         which is the vanilla module that passes things through the hull. NOT the
         vanilla Airlock MonoBehaviour, which is the fixed crew EVA lock.
         CAPTURE IS A PROCESS, NOT A SNAP, and it's interruptible. Three zones,
         all of which already exist as CarryableAttractor fields:
           MaxRange (50f)    — bay starts dragging + spinning the fighter into line
           CatchRadius (3f)  — "the distance at which objects will be captured";
                               point of no return, capture completes on its own
           BufferRange (50f) — hysteresis, "so items aren't lost quickly after
                               grabbing"; stops a break-away being re-grabbed
                               on the next frame (the yo-yo case)
           _pullVelocity     — drag speed
         Pilot can BREAK AWAY under thrust anywhere outside CatchRadius. So a
         powered bay can't yank you out of a dogfight, but it still does all the
         fiddly attitude matching — you just fly at it.
         FREE FORGE SYNERGY (verified): AttractorMaxRange and AttractorPullVelocity
         are StatTypes, and the Forge's Utility group already boosts BOTH
         (ForgeModuleState.BuildMods:240) as does the existing
         utility_extended_manipulators perk (PerkPool.cs:63, +15% attractor range).
         Tag the Fighter Bay as Utility → forging it widens the capture envelope
         and speeds the pull with ZERO new code.
         Boarding is gated by GEOMETRY not a rule: the Collar only lines up while
         latched, so an undocked fighter is physically unreachable.
         Power IS the mechanism. Powered+empty = grab state (like
         GravityScoopModule); powered+occupied = latched; cutting power = launch.
         Involuntary power loss (combat defect, or this mod's own RandomShutoff
         Maintenance Burden on the bay) = alarm + Release Grace countdown, then
         release. Launch needs TWO crew (pilot seated, crewmate cuts power) —
         deliberately coop-only content, no solo path. Recovery needs the bay
         re-powered; if the crew is dead the pilot ejects, EVAs home and does it.
      ✔ RESOLVED ON INSPECTION — "can a player ride a craft under thrust?" was
         flagged as the design-killing risk; it isn't. Vanilla turrets already seat
         players on a thrusting ship, and the mechanism is more direct than that:
         TakeoverChair has a `public SpacePlatform Platform` field, and
         SittingInChair calls SetMovingPlatform(chair.Platform.transform) when you
         sit. So riding a craft is a SUPPORTED CHAIR BEHAVIOUR, already network-
         synced (CustomPunCharacterTransformMonitor stores position platform-
         relative). Clincher: SittingInChair guards
         `if (player.Platform == ClientGame.Current.PlayerShip.Platform)` before
         applying seat modifiers — vanilla ALREADY anticipates a seated player on a
         platform that is not the player ship.
         Concrete requirement instead: the Aux Fighter needs a SpacePlatform on the
         MovingPlatform layer (CustomCharacterLocomotion gates on that layer).
         Residual risk is quality under fighter-scale accelerations, not feasibility.
      [] LARGEST REMAINING UNKNOWN is now the flight model + follow camera — both
         genuinely new code, and "is it fun?" can't be answered until it exists.
         Prototype it throwaway straight after pre-flight, before the module, chair
         or economy are built.
         DECISION: model both on the vanilla implementations rather than inventing.
         Neither can be REUSED (ShipEngine is a CellModule on the ship's grid;
         ShipExternalCamera resolves ClientGame.Current.PlayerShip in Start) but
         both are readable blueprints — details in aux-fighter-design.html:
          ShipEngine — copy the ARCHITECTURE: named input channels summed per tick
            (Dictionary<string,Vector3>, "PlayerInput" is one key), owner-gated
            integration in FixedUpdate (if IsPowered && photonView.AmOwner), per-axis
            power vectors, scaling by registered StatTypes (which is what makes it
            forge-upgradeable), AddForce/AddTorque scaled by Time.fixedDeltaTime with
            the "user is actively inputting" flag.
          !! THE SHIP IS NOT 6DOF, IT IS 2.5D !! ApplyForce builds its heading from
            ProjectOnPlane(transform.forward, Vector3.up) — forward FLATTENED to
            horizontal — and EngineTorquePower is (0,50,0), YAW ONLY. No pitch, no
            roll; vertical is a separate world-axis elevation input. Fine for a
            capital ship, almost certainly wrong for a dogfighter. Plan: copy the
            architecture exactly, change the axes deliberately (torque on all 3,
            drop the flattening so thrust follows the nose). Prototype BOTH — full
            6DOF vs the ship's model with pitch added — and fly them.
          ShipExternalCamera — Cinemachine3rdPersonFollow rig. Offset is driven by
            engine.AppliedThrust (the INPUT sum, not velocity — that's why it reacts
            the instant you touch the throttle) * 0.5, rotated into the heading and
            Lerped at Time.deltaTime*3; the ANCHOR is pushed backward by it so the
            camera trails on accel and settles on coast. Free-look via
            RotateCamera(mouseDelta) with a pitch clamp.
          !! Camera implements IKinematicObject and registers with Opsive's
            SimulationManager, so Move() runs in the CHARACTER-CONTROLLER SIM STEP,
            not Update/LateUpdate. Our pilot is an Opsive character riding the
            fighter as a moving platform — if the camera uses LateUpdate instead it
            will jitter against the seated character.
      [] NOTE: NOT a second player ship. ClientGame.Current.PlayerShip is a single
         slot read in 244 files and is never reassigned — so no HUD, objectives,
         cruise control or danger state come for free. Helm and ShipEngine are both
         CellModules on the ship's grid, so the flight model is new code.
      [] SEQUENCING: shares the Phase 8 net layer, which is only partially verified
         and was refactored 26-08-14. Finish T1-T7 first or failures have two
         candidate causes.



[] forge upgrade module leaves behind coliders

[] relic-doesn't-rotate-to-tube bug: confirmed fixed for solo/owner client.
   AnchorDock.PlaceAtAnchor/ComputeDockedPose write the correct rotation on
   both InputTarget (box) and RelicTubeTarget (relic) — same code path, no
   anchor-authoring issue. Leading suspect for the original symptom was
   CarryableObject.OnPhotonSerializeView (decompiled CarryableObject.cs:680-721):
   its reading branch only skips when Carrier != null && Carrier.AmOwner, and a
   docked relic always has Carrier == null, so non-owner clients unconditionally
   read LocalRotation off the network stream every tick and can stomp
   PlaceAtAnchor's write right after it lands. STILL NEEDS: a 2-client check
   with a non-owner client watching a relic someone else docked — that's the
   case the theory predicts could still drift. Watch for "[Forge] Pin ...
   rotation drifted" in the log (AnchorDock.cs:162-171) if it recurs.
   UPDATE (26-08-18 solo log): "Pin Relic_12_... rotation drifted 19.0°" fired
   with photonIsMine=True, photonOwner=1 — drift is happening for the OWNER
   too, so OnPhotonSerializeView's non-owner branch can't be the whole story
   (or isn't it at all in solo). Final visible rotation was still correct in
   that run, so not user-blocking, but the theory needs revisiting: something
   is nudging the relic ~19° after PlaceAtAnchor even when this client owns
   the PhotonView. Re-open once solo repro steps are known.

[] forge-buildbox-not-ready dev-workflow gotcha (26-08-18, not a code bug):
   "Void Crew > Export Asset Bundle" silently no-ops (Debug.LogError to the
   Unity console only) if the wrong thing is selected in the Project window —
   it requires exactly one folder selected, not the prefab itself. `make
   assets`/`make dev` will happily copy/build a STALE .metem_ext that predates
   a prefab edit with no error anywhere in the mod's own log. Symptom is
   "Forge BuildBox not ready yet — no vanilla BuildBox donor found" from
   ForgeSpawnCommand even though the donor-module check is unrelated. Verify
   by diffing timestamps/md5 of Exported Assets/voidcrewterminus.metem against
   Assets/voidcrewterminus.metem_ext before chasing this as an AssetLoader bug
   again.

[] relic-tube centering @done(26-08-18) (relics were landing off-center in
   their tubes. AnchorDock.PlaceAtAnchor pins the docked item's BasePivot
   exactly to anchor.position — most relics have pivotIsSelf=True (no
   authored BasePivot/CenterPivot), so Align type (Base vs Center) is a
   no-op for them either way; the fix is repositioning the RelicTubeTarget_XX
   anchor transform itself, not a code change. First attempt eyeballed the
   anchor against the wrong reference point — RelicTubeTarget_XX's ClickTarget
   child has its own authored local offset (~0.5 units) from the anchor for
   hit-box purposes only (ForgeAnchors.cs:14-15), and is NOT where the item
   docks; judging "center" against ClickTarget's gizmo/bounds instead of the
   anchor's own position looked like a ~1 relic-width miss. Fixed by nudging
   each RelicTubeTarget_XX transform directly (via the in-game Unity runtime
   editor, writing back to the prefab, then Export Asset Bundle → make assets
   → make dev) until relics land where wanted; ClickTarget left at its
   original authored offset — it doesn't need to match the anchor.)

 ✔ FIX: non-host player couldn't see/use the spawned Forge BuildBox
   @done(26-08-19) (2-client log pair confirmed it: host's log has the full
   chain — Registered BuildBox 6ba474b1... into vanilla ModuleContainer,
